T27.01|Bootstrapping builds a business using founder resources, customer receipts and internally generated cash rather than relying mainly on external equity. It can preserve ownership and discipline spending, but limits the speed and scale of investment when cash arrives slowly.~~Founder wealth and business funds should be tracked separately. Supplier credit and customer advances are financing sources with obligations, not free income.|Founder funding: Cash or assets contributed personally.;;Operating funding: Cash generated from customers.;;Working-capital funding: Timing support from advances or credit.|A founder contributes ₹10 lakh and the business receives ₹5 customer advances, then spends ₹12.~~Cash remaining is ₹3.;;The ₹5 advance still carries a delivery obligation.;;Budget the cost of fulfilling those orders.~~Cash receipts do not necessarily equal earned profit.~~Preserving ownership does not remove liquidity risk.|Separate personal and business cash.;;Track delivery obligations.;;Budget investment against realistic collections.
T27.02|A business model explains who pays, for what, and how delivery creates an economic surplus. A business plan adds assumptions, milestones, resources and financial forecasts. Market size is useful context, but unit economics and execution determine whether a specific company can profitably serve that market.~~Translate the narrative into customer counts, prices, retention, costs and cash timing. Revenue growth alone cannot validate a plan with negative contribution economics.|Subscription: Recurring access payments.;;Transaction model: Fee per completed activity.;;Product/service sale: Payment for delivered output.|A plan assumes 1,000 customers paying ₹1,000 monthly, with ₹600 variable monthly cost per customer.~~Monthly revenue is ₹10 lakh.;;Contribution is ₹4 lakh.;;Compare it with fixed costs and acquisition spending.~~The model ties customer assumptions to economic capacity.~~Churn, payment timing and scale costs require further modelling.|Identify payer and value delivered.;;Quantify unit economics.;;Connect milestones to cash requirements.
T27.03|Gross burn is cash operating outflow under a stated convention; net burn deducts relevant cash inflows. Runway estimates how long usable cash can support expected net burn. Neither profit nor revenue alone measures survival time.~~State whether capex, debt payments and one-time items are included. A simple constant-burn runway can be misleading when hiring or collections change.|Gross burn: Defined cash spending rate.;;Net burn: Spending less cash receipts.;;Runway: Cash divided by expected burn, adjusted for timing.|Usable cash is ₹30 lakh, monthly spending ₹8 and receipts ₹3.~~Net burn is ₹5 per month.;;Constant-burn runway is six months.;;If spending rises to ₹10, runway falls to about 4.3 months.~~Runway is a scenario-dependent estimate.~~Minimum cash and fundraising lead time can require earlier action.|Use usable cash.;;Forecast changing burn.;;Track commitments and financing lead times.
T27.04|Product-market fit describes evidence that a product solves a meaningful problem for a sustainable customer base. Retention, repeat use, willingness to pay and organic demand are stronger signals than vanity metrics alone. Milestones convert uncertainty into testable operating evidence.~~Growth achieved through heavy subsidies may disappear when incentives stop. Evidence should distinguish cohorts, segments and acquisition sources.|Demand evidence: Customers repeatedly seek the solution.;;Retention evidence: Users continue obtaining value.;;Economic evidence: Delivery and acquisition can be sustainable.|A startup adds 1,000 users, but only 100 remain after three months; another adds 300 and retains 240.~~Retention is 10% versus 80%.;;Compare payer conversion and contribution by cohort.;;Investigate why users return or leave.~~Headline acquisition alone does not establish product-market fit.~~These figures do not define a universal fit threshold.|Choose testable milestones.;;Follow cohorts over time.;;Separate subsidised growth from durable demand.
T27.05|Angel, seed, venture and growth rounds describe financing stages rather than guaranteed business quality. Earlier funding often finances product and market discovery; later rounds may support scaling or expansion. The appropriate capital depends on uncertainty, required resources and investor return expectations.~~Plan the next milestone and the cash needed to reach it. Raising money is an input to building the business, not proof the underlying economics work.|Angel/seed: Early experimentation and initial market evidence.;;Venture round: Growth under substantial uncertainty.;;Growth financing: Expansion of a more developed business.|A company needs ₹12 lakh to test product demand and ₹40 more to scale if the test succeeds.~~Separate the initial experiment from conditional expansion.;;Define evidence required before committing the ₹40.;;Choose financing consistent with milestones and risk.~~Staged investment can preserve flexibility.~~Delays or failed tests can create additional funding needs.|Match round to purpose.;;Budget milestone contingencies.;;Understand investor time horizon and return goals.
T27.06|A capitalisation table records ownership and securities, including shares, options and convertibles under specified assumptions. Dilution occurs when new claims reduce an existing owner's percentage or economic participation. Share count, voting power and payout rights can differ.~~Use a fully diluted view for negotiations and a separate legally issued view where needed. Option-pool increases and conversion terms can change results materially.|Issued shares: Currently outstanding ownership.;;Fully diluted shares: Includes specified potential issuance.;;Economic dilution: Change in participation after new claims.|Founders own 80 shares and investors 20; a new round issues 25 shares.~~Total becomes 125.;;Founder ownership becomes 80 ÷ 125 = 64%.;;Existing investors fall to 16%; newcomers own 20%.~~No founder shares were sold, yet percentage ownership fell.~~Preferences can further change exit proceeds.|Specify the dilution basis.;;Include options and convertibles.;;Separate voting, percentage and payout rights.
T27.07|Pre-money value is the negotiated equity value before new investment; post-money adds the new primary capital under a simplified priced-round calculation. Investor ownership equals investment divided by post-money value when there are no other adjustments. Secondary sales do not add company cash.~~Option pools, convertibles and transaction terms can change this simple ownership equation. A valuation headline should identify which securities and dilution basis it includes.|Pre-money: Equity value before primary proceeds.;;Post-money: Pre-money plus primary capital.;;Secondary transaction: Transfers existing shares without new company funding.|Assume ₹80 lakh pre-money and ₹20 new primary investment, with no other securities.~~Post-money is ₹100.;;New investor owns 20%.;;Existing holders retain 80%.~~Pre-money and post-money describe different denominators.~~A ₹20 secondary purchase would not create the same company cash inflow.|Separate primary and secondary cash.;;Check option-pool treatment.;;Reconcile ownership with actual share issuance.
T27.08|Convertible notes are debt instruments that may convert under specified terms; a SAFE is a contractual future-equity instrument under its form. Caps and discounts affect conversion pricing, while maturity and interest commonly distinguish notes from YC-style SAFEs. Other instruments and jurisdictions can differ.~~A valuation cap is not automatically the next round's valuation. Model actual definitions of capitalisation, conversion events and option pools; shorthand ownership can fail near the cap.|Convertible note: Debt with conversion provisions.;;Valuation cap: Limits conversion-price valuation basis.;;Discount: Reduces conversion price relative to a qualifying round.|For an illustrative note, round price is ₹10 per share, discount 20%, and cap-derived price ₹6; terms use the lower price.~~Discount price is ₹8.;;Conversion price is ₹6.;;₹6 lakh principal converts into 1 lakh shares, ignoring interest.~~The cap binds in this case.~~Use the actual agreement; a different form can produce different results.|Read conversion definitions.;;Model interest and pools.;;Compare ownership across round scenarios.
T27.09|A term sheet outlines proposed investment economics, governance and protections. Some provisions may be binding while others depend on final documents. Price is only one element: liquidation rights, board seats, consent rights and information rights affect control and outcomes.~~Evaluate provisions as a package. A higher headline valuation with restrictive economics can be less attractive than a lower valuation with simpler terms.|Economic terms: Price, preferences and participation.;;Control terms: Board and reserved decisions.;;Process terms: Exclusivity, diligence and closing conditions.|Two offers each invest ₹20 lakh at the same price; one has a 1× non-participating preference, the other 2× participating.~~Model low and high exit payouts.;;Compare consent and governance rights.;;Review binding sections and completion conditions.~~Equal valuation does not imply equal financing economics.~~Legal interpretation depends on final documents and jurisdiction.|Model payout rights.;;Review control provisions.;;Clarify binding and non-binding terms.
T27.10|Liquidation preferences specify priority payouts in defined exit events. Non-participating preferred investors typically choose their preference or conversion value; participating structures may receive a preference and then share remaining proceeds, subject to any cap. The exact waterfall determines outcomes.~~Ownership percentages alone cannot explain startup exits. Multiple rounds can have senior, pari passu or other negotiated ranking.|Non-participating: Preference or converted common value.;;Participating: Preference plus residual sharing under terms.;;Seniority: Order among preferred claims.|Assume one investor paid ₹20 lakh for 20%, with 1× non-participating preference, and exit equity proceeds ₹50.~~Preference payout is ₹20.;;Conversion value is ₹10.;;Investor chooses ₹20, leaving ₹30 for common holders.~~The investor receives more than its headline ownership share.~~This assumes no debt, costs or other preferred classes.|Model every class.;;Read participation and caps.;;Distinguish enterprise sale price from equity proceeds.
T27.11|Anti-dilution provisions adjust preferred conversion terms after certain lower-priced issuances. Full-ratchet and weighted-average methods offer different protection; ordinary percentage dilution from a fairly priced new round is a separate concept. Protection shifts some down-round economic burden to other holders.~~Actual formulas depend on eligible securities, exemptions and the defined capitalisation base. Do not assume every share or option triggers the same treatment.|Full ratchet: Resets conversion price to an eligible lower price.;;Weighted average: Adjusts using price and issuance size.;;Exempt issuance: Contractually excluded from adjustment.|A preferred investment of ₹20 lakh initially converts at ₹10 per share; assume full-ratchet reset to ₹5.~~Initial conversion entitlement is 2 lakh shares.;;Adjusted entitlement is 4 lakh.;;Recalculate all holders' fully diluted percentages.~~Protection can increase dilution for unprotected holders.~~Weighted-average terms generally require the specific formula and share base.|Identify covered issuances.;;Use the actual formula.;;Rebuild the complete cap table.
T27.12|Vesting links ownership or option entitlement to continued service or milestones. Founder vesting can protect the team when a founder leaves early; employee options create a potential ownership claim with exercise terms. Options are not the same as immediately owned shares or cash compensation.~~Exercise price, expiry, leaver provisions and tax treatment affect value. The option pool also influences dilution and hiring capacity.|Time vesting: Earned through service duration.;;Cliff: Initial threshold before vesting begins.;;Option exercise: Paying the specified price to obtain shares.|Assume 48,000 options vest evenly over 48 months after satisfying the agreement's initial conditions.~~Monthly vesting is 1,000 options.;;After 24 eligible months, 24,000 are vested.;;At ₹5 exercise price, exercising those costs ₹1.2 lakh.~~Vested entitlement still requires consideration of exercise and liquidity.~~Real cliffs, acceleration and local tax rules may alter outcomes.|Read vesting and leaver terms.;;Model exercise costs.;;Include the pool in dilution analysis.
T27.13|Venture debt lends to businesses whose value may depend heavily on future growth and fundraising. It can extend runway with less immediate equity issuance, but adds interest, repayment and covenant risk. Warrants or fees can still create economic dilution.~~Debt should bridge a credible cash or financing path rather than conceal an unsustainable burn rate. A missed fundraising milestone can make repayment particularly difficult.|Term loan: Scheduled borrowing and repayment.;;Revenue-linked financing: Payments tied to defined receipts.;;Warrant feature: Potential equity participation for the lender.|A startup has ₹10 lakh cash and burns ₹5 monthly; it borrows ₹15 with ₹1.5 annual interest.~~Cash rises to ₹25 before fees.;;Simple burn-only runway becomes five months.;;Include interest and principal dates in a cash forecast.~~Borrowing extends liquidity but creates future obligations.~~Runway is shorter if repayment starts immediately.|Model debt service dates.;;Review covenants and warrants.;;Stress a delayed or failed equity raise.
T27.14|Fundraising connects the company's capital needs and investment case with suitable investors. Good communication explains progress, risks, use of proceeds and what evidence the funding will produce. A persuasive deck must reconcile with the operating model and data room.~~Begin before cash is exhausted; diligence and approvals take uncertain time. Existing investor updates should be factual and consistent rather than changing definitions to flatter performance.|Investment narrative: Problem, economics and opportunity.;;Funding plan: Amount, timing and milestones.;;Investor reporting: Evidence, changes and risks.|A company has eight months of runway and expects a four-month financing process plus two-month contingency.~~Start with at least six months needed for the process.;;Tie proposed proceeds to specific milestones.;;Prepare alternatives if timing slips.~~Fundraising timing is part of liquidity management.~~Expected closing dates are not committed cash.|Reconcile deck and model.;;Prepare verifiable operating data.;;Communicate misses and contingency plans.
T27.15|Startup economics differ by model. SaaS focuses on recurring revenue, retention and serving costs; marketplaces on gross transaction value, take rate and liquidity; consumer businesses on repeat purchasing and fulfilment; fintech on regulated activity, losses and transaction economics. Revenue definitions must match the model.~~Do not compare gross merchandise value directly with another company's recognised revenue. Growth quality depends on contribution, retention and cash requirements.|SaaS: Recurring access and cohort retention.;;Marketplace: Facilitates transactions for a take rate.;;Consumer/fintech: Delivery economics or financial-service risk.|A marketplace processes ₹100 lakh GMV at 10% take rate and spends ₹6 on transaction costs and incentives.~~Illustrative net-platform revenue is ₹10.;;Contribution is ₹4 before fixed costs.;;Check principal-versus-agent reporting separately.~~GMV is not automatically revenue or profit.~~Definitions and accounting treatment require the actual contract.|Choose model-specific metrics.;;Reconcile gross and net measures.;;Track cohorts, loss rates and cash conversion.
T27.16|A down round issues equity at a lower valuation or price than a preceding round. Bridge financing funds a transition to another milestone; recapitalisation restructures ownership or claims. These transactions may preserve the business while materially changing investor and founder outcomes.~~Valuation decline is only part of the story. Preferences, anti-dilution, new money requirements and governance can redistribute value.|Down round: Lower-priced equity financing.;;Bridge: Interim financing toward a defined event.;;Recapitalisation: Reworks capital and ownership terms.|A company needs ₹20 lakh at ₹30 pre-money after a prior ₹80 valuation, with no other adjustments.~~New post-money is ₹50.;;New investor receives 40%.;;Recalculate existing holdings and any contractual protections.~~Survival capital can cause substantial dilution.~~A lower valuation alone does not describe the full waterfall.|Model all outstanding securities.;;Test whether the bridge reaches a viable milestone.;;Explain the new control and payout terms.
T27.17|Startup exits include sales, listings and secondary transfers, but each creates different cash and liquidity outcomes. Exit waterfalls distribute available equity proceeds according to debt, costs, preferences and conversion rights. A company valuation is not the founder's cash receipt.~~A listing may impose sale restrictions, while a secondary transfer can benefit selected holders without funding the company. Track who sells and what obligations remain.|Trade sale: Business sold to another owner.;;IPO: Shares admitted to public trading.;;Secondary exit: Existing interests transferred.|An exit has ₹100 lakh enterprise proceeds, debt ₹25 and costs ₹5; assume only common equity.~~Equity proceeds are ₹70.;;A 30% founder receives ₹21.;;Add preference calculations if preferred securities exist.~~Headline transaction size exceeds distributable equity cash.~~Tax and sale restrictions are excluded and must be analysed separately.|Build the enterprise-to-equity bridge.;;Apply the actual waterfall.;;Separate valuation, liquidity and after-tax receipts.
